NNAAMMEE
     ttffttppdd — Trivial File Transfer Protocol daemon

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ttffttppdd [--4466ccddiivvww] [--ll _a_d_d_r_e_s_s] [--pp _p_o_r_t] [--rr _s_o_c_k_e_t] _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ttffttppdd serves _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y contents via the Trivial File Transfer Protocol.

     The use of tftp(1) does not require an account or password on the remote
     system.  Due to the lack of authentication information, ttffttppdd will allow
     only publicly readable files to be accessed.  By default files may only
     be read, unless the --ww option is specified.  Files may be written only if
     they already exist and are publicly writable, unless the --cc flag is spec‐
     ified.  Note that this extends the concept of “public” to include all
     users on all hosts that can be reached through the network; this may not
     be appropriate on all systems, and its implications should be considered
     before enabling TFTP service.  ttffttppdd always provides random data at the
     path _/_e_t_c_/_r_a_n_d_o_m_._s_e_e_d, and therefore this path will be ignored inside the
     _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y.  OpenBSD network bootloaders access this path to harvest en‐
     tropy during kernel load.

     ttffttppdd needs to start as root, then calls chroot(2) into _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y, and
     drops privileges to the “_tftpd” user.

     The options are as follows:

     --44      Forces ttffttppdd to use IPv4 addresses only.

     --66      Forces ttffttppdd to use IPv6 addresses only.

     --cc      Allow new files to be created; otherwise uploaded files must al‐
             ready exist.  Files are created with default permissions allowing
             anyone to read or write to them.

             This option implies --ww.

     --dd      Do not daemonize.  If this option is specified, ttffttppdd will run in
             the foreground and log to stderr instead of syslog(3).  This im‐
             plies --vv.

     --ii      Look up the requested path in the subdirectory named after the
             client's IP address.  For read requests, if the file is not
             found, ttffttppdd falls back on the requested path.  Note that no at‐
             tempt is made to limit the client to its subdirectory.  This op‐
             tion cannot be combined with --rr.

     --ll _a_d_d_r_e_s_s
             Listen on the specified address.  By default ttffttppdd listens on
             wildcard addresses.

     --pp _p_o_r_t
             Listen on the specified port.  By default ttffttppdd listens on the
             port indicated in the ‘tftp’ service description; see
             services(5).

     --rr _s_o_c_k_e_t
             Issue filename rewrite requests to the specified UNIX domain
             socket.  ttffttppdd will write lines in the format "IP OP filename",
             terminated by a newline, where IP is the client's IP address, and
             OP is one of "read" or "write".  ttffttppdd expects replies in the
             format "filename" terminated by a newline.  All rewrite requests
             from the daemon must be answered (even if it is with the original
             filename) before the TFTP request will continue.  By default
             ttffttppdd does not use filename rewriting.  This option cannot be
             combined with --ii.

     --vv      Log the client IP, type of request, and filename.

     --ww      Allow files to be written to.

